provisioners:
=============

Provisioners in Terraform are used to execute scripts or commands on a local machine or a remote machine during resource creation or destruction.

For example, after creating an EC2 instance, you can use a provisioner to install Docker or configure a web server.

1. Types of Provisioners
--------------------------
Terraform commonly uses these provisioners:

local-exec — Executes commands on the machine running Terraform.
remote-exec — Executes commands on a remote machine, such as an EC2 instance.
file — Copies files or directories from the local machine to a remote machine.

Examples:
---------
a). Local-Exec:-

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = "ami-xxxxxxxx"
  instance_type = "t3.micro"

  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "echo EC2 instance created"
  }
}

Use case: Logging, running local scripts, or triggering another process.

b). Remote-Exec Example:- Executes commands on a remote EC2 instance over SSH.

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = "ami-xxxxxxxx"
  instance_type = "t3.micro"
  key_name      = "ram"

  provisioner "remote-exec" {
    inline = [
      "sudo yum update -y",
      "sudo yum install httpd -y",
      "sudo systemctl start httpd"
    ]

    connection {
      type        = "ssh"
      user        = "ec2-user"
      private_key = file("ram.pem")
      host        = self.public_ip
    }
  }
}

Use case: Installing packages or running setup commands on an EC2 instance.

c). File Provisioner:- Copies a local file to a remote EC2 instance.

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = "ami-xxxxxxxx"
  instance_type = "t3.micro"
  key_name      = "ram"

  provisioner "file" {
    source      = "app.sh"
    destination = "/tmp/app.sh"

    connection {
      type        = "ssh"
      user        = "ec2-user"
      private_key = file("ram.pem")
      host        = self.public_ip
    }
  }
}

usecase: This example copies app.sh from your local machine to /tmp/app.sh on the EC2 instance.


note:  Terraform recommends using provisioners only when necessary. Prefer cloud-init, EC2 user data, configuration-management tools, 
       or purpose-built resources when they can accomplish the same task more reliably.
